  ('083f2297-a491-56e4-bdd7-99d95a12ec82', 'c5c7a446-ac88-5c6b-8b82-3a4530e35f5f', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Practical application$gs$, $gs$Read the key passage again in context. Write a short paragraph summarising what you learned, then choose one realistic action that reflects the teaching. If something remains unclear, write down the question and discuss it with your teacher or church leader. TEACHER AND FACILITATOR GUIDE How to teach each lesson • Start by asking learners what they already know about the subject. Do not assume prior theological knowledge. • Read the Bible passage aloud and explain its setting before explaining individual phrases. • Teach the definition first. Then explain the idea in short steps and give a familiar example. • Pause to check understanding. Invite learners to explain the main point in their own words. • Use reflection questions for honest thought, not to shame or pressure learners to disclose private matters. • Use the comprehensive check to identify what needs reteaching. Correct misunderstandings gently and return to the Bible passage. • End with a practical response that is realistic and connected to the lesson. Suggested assessment approach A learner has understood a lesson when they can (1) define its main idea, (2) explain the Bible passage in context, (3) distinguish the teaching from a common misunderstanding, and (4) describe a practical response. For open-ended questions, assess clarity and biblical understanding rather than requiring identical wording. Important editorial note This simplified edition preserves the curriculum’s module and lesson titles and supplies a clear teaching framework for every lesson. Some Christian traditions interpret particular subjects—especially baptism, assurance, and aspects of end-times teaching—differently. Teachers should explain their church’s position fairly, distinguish clear biblical statements from areas of disagreement, and encourage learners to read Scripture in context.$gs$)
